{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassTheory.H3MarkovWenzlWeights","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassTheory.H3MarkovWenzlWeights","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.h3_markov_wenzl_weights","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.h3_markov_wenzl_weights","kind":"theorem","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/h3-markov-wenzl-weights.lean#L69-L80","doc":"**Markov normalisation at q = 1** for the closed-form H_3 Wenzl\nweights `(y_(3), y_(2,1), y_(1^3))|_{q=1} = (1/2, 1/4, 0)` with\ndimensions `(d_(3), d_(2,1), d_(1^3)) = (1, 2, 1)`:\n\n  Σ_λ y_λ(1) · d_λ = (1/2)·1 + (1/4)·2 + 0·1 = 1.\n\nThe weights are NOT uniform — `y_(1^3)(q) ≡ 0` (Temperley-Lieb\nquotient: the Jones polynomial factors through TL_3, killing the\n3-row partition), and `y_(3)(1) = 1/2 ≠ 1/4 = y_(2,1)(1)`. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:h3-markov-wenzl-weights","label":"prop:h3-markov-wenzl-weights","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/h3-markov-wenzl-weights.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.h3_tl_quotient_identity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.h3_tl_quotient_identity","kind":"theorem","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/h3-markov-wenzl-weights.lean#L55-L67","doc":"**Temperley-Lieb quotient kernel identity** for H_3:\n`[2]³ − 2[2] = [4]` in the symmetric q-integer convention\n(`[2] = q + q⁻¹`, `[4] = q³ + q + q⁻¹ + q⁻³`).\n\nThis identity is the algebraic content of `y_(1^3) ≡ 0`: with the\nsign-channel weight identically zero, the Markov-normalisation\nresidual `1 − 2/[2]²` assigned to the trivial channel equals\n`[4]/[2]³` exactly.  Equivalently: `([2]² − 2)/[2]² = [4]/[2]³`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}